Task 1: Philosopher Class

The Philosopher class was fully implemented by completing the eat(), think(), talk(), and run() methods. A Status enum was added to keep track of each philosopher’s current activity.
The eating, thinking, and talking actions now include appropriate output messages, thread yielding, and random sleep intervals to simulate real philosopher behavior.
The run() method was updated to manage the full philosopher cycle of picking up chopsticks, eating, putting down chopsticks, thinking, and requesting permission to talk through the monitor.

Task 2: Monitor

The Monitor class was implemented using Java synchronization mechanisms (synchronized, wait(), and notifyAll()) to coordinate access to shared resources.
A chopstick tracking array was added to monitor whether each chopstick is available or in use, allowing philosophers to only pick up chopsticks when both required ones are available.
The pickUp() and putDown() methods manage chopstick allocation and release, while requestTalk() and endTalk() ensure that only one philosopher can talk at a time.
This implementation provides safe synchronization and prevents conflicts between philosopher threads.

Task 3: Variable Number of Philosophers

The DiningPhilosophers class was modified to allow the number of philosophers to be provided through a command-line argument. The program now checks whether an argument was provided, 
uses the default number if none is given, and validates that the provided value is a positive integer. Invalid input is handled by displaying an error message and usage instructions. 
This allows the simulation to dynamically create the requested number of philosopher threads instead of being limited to the default value.
